  {
    "episode_id": 74292,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/4/74292.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 5,
    "turn_text": "Well, I guess it has to do a lot with sort of my formative years as a young person growing up. I'd always been a sci-fi geek. And to watch as our nation has evolved its technological capabilities into the virtual world, one that we've all had to quickly adopt in light of the pandemic I became very interested in what drives our use of technology, why we adopted it, and how it can be a real asset to our lives and how it can be harmful to our lives. And I saw that as a space that I really like to know more about, be curious about, and that curiosity led me into sort of a deeper understanding of the interplay of our private sector corporations that provide a lot of our technological products, the ones that we use each and every day, and what that means for inclusion and diversity. And when you have a virtual world, that we live part of our lives in. And you're in a physical world, if you will, that has had a history of discrimination. It's not a leap to believe that some of that bias in discrimination would be transferable and transferred into our virtual world. And so I thought that it was critical that we examine this and that we stop it in its tracks so that this is not something that discrimination and bias aren't perpetuated in a way that we know causes harm to communities of color and to the greater society. Because when we discriminate, We are not bringing forth as much human productivity as we possibly can. We're not maximizing on all the talent that we have resident and different bodies that make this nation so formidable, so great. But we continue to lag behind in our progress as a result of that.",
