  {
    "episode_id": 74292,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/4/74292.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 7,
    "turn_text": "Absolutely. Add to the racial reckoning and the health imperative, the economic imperative, right? So many people have been laid off as usually, you know, last one hired, first one fired, when you look at the hardship of those who are underemployed and being able to take care of their daily responsibilities, whether it be their rent, buying homes, all of this sort of has converged. And when you think about how we have to apply for everything now online, you know, the implications for Americans who are hired for their dream job, you know, algorithms can determine that. approve for home mortgage or even sent to prison. Algorithms are playing an outsized role in all of that. And the problem is that these algorithms can be dangerously biased and result in discriminatory decisions. And so that's why it is, so let me unpack that a little. Human beings are responsible for programming and creating algorithms. If that human being's experience is limited or has been jaded in any way by an ideological philosophy and that person based on those assumptions, program, code, those algorithms will be a reflection of that person's human experience. And so that is why AI is so important in the lives of every American. And we'll grow in its importance as we continue to bake into everything we do, some measure of technological data mining or information that is used to make decisions. And so that's why this area is very important for us to really create a subject matter around. We've yet to really, really do that. And that's part of why I see my voice as being important in this conversation.",
