  {
    "episode_id": 74292,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/4/74292.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 16,
    "turn_text": "Well, I think that this is sort of a new industry. And we need to call it what it is. It's an industry, just like the automobile industry, just like so many other industries that we have come to rely on, our agricultural industry, The great thing about technology is that it crosses every industry. And as we look at this moment where I think crossing a Rubicon into a far more technologically advanced civil society, when we look at the imperatives before us, you spoke about my smart cities caucus work, That's not the wave of the future. That's the wave of now. We're looking at ways in which we establish micro grids for energy conservation. We're looking at how we establish charging stations in communities of color. All of that will ride upon an infrastructure based in technology. opportunities for entrepreneurship of their opportunities for new jobs, and so young people, this is your thing. You wake up using your smartphones each and every day, and your curiosity about what drives that will drive you into the rabbit hole of understanding how you fit into that ecosystem and what you can do to improve it, what you can do to realize it in your lifetime and build out something enduring for generations to come. Young people, regardless of race, ethnicity, religion, are all on the same wavelength. when it comes to the use of technology, why not be go beyond being a user to being a creator? And that's sort of what I'm enthusiastic about, you know, how can we use our creativity and innovation, our imagination, marry that with the science of technology and, you know, provide for not only the United States of America, but the world, because we're talking about a global infrastructure to a certain extent, that will advance humankind, will make life far more efficient and effective in strengthening us as a nation, as communities, as families. So I want to encourage young people to fear not, but go boldly. and adopt a technology based mind frame that centers your experience into the framework of developing new and bold technologies.",
