    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/4/74292.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 21,
    "turn_text": "Well, the rose for me is, again, the creativity I share with everyone, I'm left handed. And so that married with my sci-fi passion, I believe that, you know, what we believe we can achieve. And that has been demonstrated time and time again. And so I want to encourage, again, that creativity. You know, we can build out stronger communities. We can connect rural communities. We can provide energy efficiency. We can mitigate the damages of climate change, all of that can be done. We just have to have the will. We have the tools and we have the technology to do it. Number two, the thorn is if we sit back and don't challenge the status quo, the things that have inhibited us in the physical world will migrate and get hardened in the virtual world in the technology space. And that would be a shame because we know better. We know better. And essentially, we would be stopping the clock on our advancement as a civil society if we don't address that now. If we let prophets in greed be our only motivation, then we will be subjected to really a lack of vision around how we protect ourselves in this space. That blind spot can mean harm, can mean danger, can mean death. And we've seen that play out already. What I hope for the future is that we widen the aperture wide enough to embrace all of the talent, that we make it a real discipline in our elementary schools through higher ed so that there's an expertise pipeline that is constantly fed by the diversity of who we are And then we give everyone that opportunity. And that we build a trade out of utilizing technology, maintaining it, sustaining it. You don't have to have a college degree in many instances in order to participate in this revolutionary utility that we have before us. called technology, and that we will use every avenue at our disposal to build up the type of robust human resource to, again, take us beyond the 21st century with the tools, the expertise that we need to make a long lasting impact on the world. On our world here in the United States of America, but indeed globally where so many others don't have access yet to what we have as a game changer. And my hope is that one day we will all see and say Wakanda forever.",
